course: dictionary
lesson: 6619
title: Nosebleeds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A nosebleed is blood coming out of your nose. It often happens because one tiny blood vessel inside your nose breaks. «For a nosebleed, lean forward, not back, and pinch the soft part of the nose.» Those are the first steps to take.
explain: Nosebleeds are common in children and are usually not serious. They can come from picking your nose, dry air, a knock, or a cold. «Frequent nosebleeds in children usually come from one fragile vessel, picking or dry air, such as heated air in winter, and are rarely serious.»
explain: Tilting your head back is a mistake. «Do not tilt your head back in a nosebleed, because blood runs down your throat and can make you sick.» Sit down, lean forward, and breathe through your mouth instead.
explain: Know when to get help. «A nosebleed that will not stop after 20 minutes, or that came after a big knock, needs medical help.» Tell an adult straight away.
fact: A nosebleed means blood coming out of your nose.
fact: You can say «have a nosebleed», «get a nosebleed» or «stop a nosebleed».
fact: To help, lean forward, pinch the soft part of your nose for about 10 minutes, and breathe through your mouth.
fact: Spit out any blood that runs into your mouth, and do not swallow it.
fact: Get help if the bleeding does not stop after about 20 minutes, or after a bad bump to the head.
exercise: «For a nosebleed, lean forward, not back, and pinch the soft part of the nose.» What should you do with your head? | Hold it up high | *Lean forward | Tilt it back | Lie flat on the floor
exercise: What does a nosebleed mean? | A sore throat | A cut on your hand | A cold that makes you sneeze | *Blood coming out of your nose
exercise: «A nosebleed that will not stop after 20 minutes needs medical help.» Find and copy the word that names the problem. | medical | *nosebleed | minutes | needs
test: «Do not tilt your head back in a nosebleed, because blood runs down your throat.» Why should you not tilt your head back? | It makes your head feel cool | It stops the bleeding straight away | *Blood could run down your throat and make you sick | It helps you breathe through your nose
test: When should you tell an adult about a nosebleed? | Never, it is always fine | Only when it is all over | As soon as you feel a little tickle | *If it has not stopped after about 20 minutes
exam: Sam has a nosebleed after a big bump to the head. What should he do? | Wait for it to stop by itself and tell nobody | *Sit down, lean forward, pinch his nose and get help from an adult | Run around until it stops | Tilt his head back and lie down
